{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Impact, stated first\n\nEvery live Reconcile Bridge pass re-attempts a set of inbound (Jira -> rebar) link writes that **cannot ever succeed**: the source ticket is closed, the relationship is redundant with the hierarchy, or the link would create a cycle. Each attempt fails, is logged at WARNING, and is forgotten. The next pass derives the identical set and tries again. The bridge therefore **never reaches a quiet steady state**, and its log carries a permanent floor of errors that must be mentally filtered before a real fault is visible.\n\n## Evidence (measured 2026-08-12, live workflow logs)\n\nFour consecutive live Reconcile Bridge runs — 31568815075, 31569179421, 31569723185, 31570037358, spanning ~30 minutes — each emit exactly **19** failed inbound link writes: 5 `is closed`, 13 `redundant link — ancestor-descendant`, 1 `would create a cycle at epic level`. Normalising the log lines and diffing two of those passes yields a **byte-identical** failing set (same source, target, relation, reason). Passes re-dispatch every ~5-6 min, so this is roughly 200+ known-doomed link writes per hour, indefinitely.\n\n**Correction to the original filing:** these are *local* `rebar link` subprocess writes, not Jira round-trips (the log shows `rebar link failed (exit 1)`). The waste is real (19 doomed subprocess spawns per pass) but it is CPU/log cost, not Jira API cost. AC4 below is restated accordingly.\n\n## Confirmed mechanism\n\n- **Emitter:** `inbound_differ._diff_links_inbound` dedups a candidate ADD only against the ticket's *current* local deps (`existing_deps = ld.deps_as_set(local_ticket)`). A failed link never lands a LINK event, so the pair is never in `existing_deps`, so the identical ADD record is re-emitted every pass.\n- **Applier:** `apply_inbound_records._inbound_update_apply_links`, `add` branch — `try: rebar.link(...) except Exception: logger.warning(...)`. That warn-and-continue is the whole of the handling. No retry counter, no suppression, no durable trace.\n- **Why the failures are permanent:** all three are raised by `rebar/graph/_links.py::add_dependency` from reduced local state — redundancy (`is_redundant` -> ValueError), cycle (`CyclicDependencyError`), and closed source (ValueError). They are deterministic validation verdicts, not I/O or transient faults, and re-raise identically until the *local structure* changes.\n- **No durable memory anywhere:** the binding store (`.bridge_state/bindings.json`, v2) holds only jira_key/state/baseline/peer-parent; `peer_state` baselines five scalar fields and explicitly not links; `managed_refs` records only refs that were successfully created; `last_pass.json` is pass-level. Nothing records an attempt or a failure.\n\n## Approach\n\nGive the applier a durable, self-healing memory of *permanently impossible* inbound link ADDs, and consult it before spending the write.\n\n1. **New module** `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/impossible_links.py`:\n   - `classify(exc) -> reason | None` — deterministic classification of the three permanent shapes (`closed_source`, `redundant_ancestry`, `cycle`). Anything else returns `None` and behaviour is unchanged (fail-open preserved for transient/unknown faults). This is the safety valve: only shapes we can prove are structural get recorded.\n   - `deciding_digest(source, target, relation, repo_root)` — a stable digest over the *structural* inputs those validators read: for each endpoint, its status, its full parent chain, and its sorted dep set. This is the invalidation key: reopen the source, reparent an endpoint, or break the offending link and the digest changes, so the record no longer matches and the link is re-attempted exactly once more.\n   - A JSON store at `.tickets-tracker/.bridge_state/impossible_links.json` (`version: 1`), keyed `source|target|relation` -> `{reason, digest, first_seen, last_seen, attempts}`. A **separate file** from `bindings.json` on purpose: no schema collision with the binding store or with the peer-confirmation record planned by epic despotic-floriated-leopard.\n2. **Applier change** (`_inbound_update_apply_links`, `add` branch only): before calling `rebar.link`, if a record matches this triple *and* its digest still matches, skip the write. Otherwise attempt as today; on failure, classify, and record only when the reason is permanent.\n3. **Operator visibility:** a newly-recorded impossible link still logs at WARNING exactly once (so a genuinely new one is loud). Subsequent skips log at DEBUG, plus one WARNING summary line per pass naming the count and the store path. Deleting the JSON file re-qualifies everything — the manual clear.\n\n**Deliberately out of scope:** the REMOVE branch, `_inbound_unlink_one`, and the differ's G3/G4/G5 suppression — that surface belongs to epic despotic-floriated-leopard (never-pushed managed links vs peer deletions). This change touches only the ADD branch and adds a distinct state file, so the two are complementary, not conflicting.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `impossible_links.classify` returns the correct reason for each of the three real error texts captured above, and `None` for a transient/unknown exception (unit test, one case per shape).\n- [ ] With a matching record present and its digest unchanged, `_inbound_update_apply_links` does **not** call `rebar.link` for that triple and does not count it in `links_applied` (unit test asserting the facade is never invoked).\n- [ ] When the deciding inputs change (e.g. the closed source ticket is reopened), the digest no longer matches and the link **is** re-attempted (unit test that mutates status and asserts one fresh attempt).\n- [ ] A failure whose reason is not one of the three permanent shapes is **not** recorded, and is retried on the next pass exactly as today (unit test — no behaviour regression for transient faults).\n- [ ] A first-time impossible link logs at WARNING (operator-visible); a subsequent skip does not (unit test on caplog levels).\n- [ ] Steady-state evidence, not log suppression: a second `_inbound_update_apply_links` call over the same unchanged payload and store performs **zero** `rebar.link` invocations for the three shapes (unit test counting facade calls across two passes: first pass N, second pass 0).\n- [ ] The record survives a process restart — it is read back from `.bridge_state/impossible_links.json` by a fresh store instance (unit test).\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the existing inbound-link test files (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_applier_inbound_links.py`, `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_link_removal_2b16.py`) all pass unchanged."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786516859004944001,"uuid":"fe95a7ed-7fa9-4519-b47e-c5c4f3942dcc"}